  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain over the SW won't be enough to cause \nflooding over the next 24-36 hrs, but the risk returns Sat night\nwith deep convection and possbile heavy rain. Then the risk \nexpands to the entire CWA for Sun-Sun night.\n\nRain is along a weak boundary and flowing right over the wettest\narea of the CWA. Thankfully, it is light and expected to remain\nthat way as no ltg has been observed upstream and it should stay\nas stable as it has been.\n\nThere is just a tiny risk of one of the cu out in the rest of\nthe CWA poking through the cap and making a few raindrops. Have\nonly spotty PoPs for that possibility, and mainly in the NE. \n\nAs moisture increases ahead of a very mature cyclone crossing \nthe Great Lakes on Sat night, overrunning precip will develop.\nThe rain will be likely be over the SW where it is already the\nwetter place in the CWA. FFGs are currently above 1.25\"/hr, and\na mainly dry time Sat should help those rise a little. But, as\nwe get closer in time, we will have to consider a (flash) flood\nwatch for Sun. Chose to hold off at this point due to the time\nrange and uncertainty with how heavy and repetitive the\nrain/storms will be later this weekend, and especially how wide\nof an area to cover with a watch. ERO from WPC hydro forecasters\nhas nearly all of the CWA (nrn tier = not yet) in the SLGT risk\ncategory for Day 3 (Sun).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Fog tonight.\n\nAs we clear out tonight, some fog is expected as Fri AM. We \naren't expecting it to be any better or worse than last night.\nThe valleys will be more likely to have fog, and down in the SW\nwhere there is going to be more ambient moisture.",
